Reconstruct the fifth common physics block as an argument. Begin with a signed charge ledger and evidence for interaction. State the static vacuum point-source premises, build vector fields from signed contributions, and distinguish source field from probe force and acceleration. Derive potential differences and work using the finite-source path derivative, with source-avoiding paths and an explicit reference. Explain why a zero field at one point, a zero chosen potential and a neutral total charge are different claims.

Develop current from accumulated signed charge and derive finite boundary charge balance before neglecting storage at a junction. Establish the port voltage/current orientation and absorbed power vi. Starting from constant Q=Cv, derive the capacitor energy rather than importing a field-energy formula whose prerequisites are missing. State what changes when C varies, the response is nonlinear, or leakage introduces another path. Treat Ohmic resistance as a material premise and lumped circuits as a scoped approximation.

Derive the two-capacitor redistribution solution from the charge sum and voltage difference. Prove uniqueness, calculate signed transfer and show that the decrease in capacitor energy equals integrated resistor power. Check the original exact intermediate state and limiting values independently. Then prove the voltage-only parameter ambiguity for the entire history, and derive the altered charge and energy balances when a finite input resistance loads the second node. Explain the difference between an added observation and an observation that changes the model.

Complete the bounded readings assigned through [University Physics Volume 2 — OpenStax](../electromagnetism/sources/University-Physics-Volume-2.lingua#r_5JQ0CPQ0RNJNVH175Y9V305J5Q) and the shared electromagnetic Records, progressing to their full exercises when the mathematics is available. The fresh inspection covers selected text and equations, not every figure, dataset or worked source example. The original finite-source and charge-sharing programs are independent teaching investigations rather than reproductions of a published experiment. A proposed supervised measurement remains proposed until evidence has actually been collected and analyzed.

For independent work, choose a different benign finite source arrangement or positive-component charge network. Establish the domain and initial data, derive predictions, construct a distinct check and identify a model rival. State exactly which observation would distinguish it. Defend the mathematical result and the physical assumptions separately; retain a reviewer's criticism and a justified revision. A later practical reconstruction must also account for source geometry, material state, time resolution, loading, calibration and uncertainty.

The physics core remains unfinished. Broader rotations and continua, field/flux/induction theory, entropy/statistical accounts, quantum and relativistic transitions, astronomy, demanding experimental reconstruction and advanced independent work all remain in scope. This block prepares chemistry's electron/charge and chemical–electrical accounts and engineering's later electrical realization; it does not complete those branches. Continue chemistry across the fifth common pass before returning to specialized leaves. The full six-area coverage review follows the corresponding computing, engineering and inquiry blocks.

